{"name":"Cold Air Turquoise","hex":"#c1dcdb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a low temperature.","Causing the air to be cold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A condition of low temperature.","A harsh place; a place of abandonment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cold"},{"term":"air","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Airoran."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance constituting Earth's atmosphere: a gaseous mixture of nitrogen, oxygen, and various trace gases.","The substance of the atmosphere seen as an agency of freshness."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/air"},{"term":"turquoise","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sky-blue, greenish-blue, or greenish-gray semi-precious gemstone.","A pale greenish-blue colour, like that of the gemstone."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of turquoise (the gemstone).","Having a pale greenish-blue colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/turquoise"}],"entries":[{"title":"Air Turquoise","blocks":[{"text":"Air Turquoise was a low-cost airline based in France. It operated services to Marseille, Nice and Bordeaux from Reims Champagne Airport. The airline was liquidated on 2 August 2006."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Air_Turquoise_logo.png/330px-Air_Turquoise_logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_Turquoise"},{"title":"Cold","blocks":[{"text":"Cold is the presence of low temperature, especially in the atmosphere. In common usage, cold is often a subjective perception. A lower bound to temperature is absolute zero, defined as 0.00 K on the Kelvin scale, an absolute thermodynamic temperature scale. This corresponds to −273.15 °C on the Celsius scale, −459.67 °F on the Fahrenheit scale, and 0.00 °R on the Rankine scale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg/330px-Antarctic_iceberg%2C_2001_-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cold"},{"title":"Atmosphere of Earth","blocks":[{"text":"The atmosphere of Earth consists of a layer of mixed gas that is retained by gravity, surrounding the Earth's surface. It contains variable quantities of suspended aerosols and particulates that create weather features such as clouds and hazes. The atmosphere serves as a protective buffer between the Earth's surface and outer space. It shields the surface from most meteoroids and ultraviolet solar radiation, reduces diurnal temperature variation – the temperature extremes between day and night, and keeps it warm through heat retention via the greenhouse effect. The atmosphere redistributes heat and moisture among different regions via air currents, and provides the chemical and climate conditions that allow life to exist and evolve on Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg/330px-Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atmosphere_of_Earth"},{"title":"Turquoise","blocks":[{"text":"Turquoise is an opaque, blue-to-green mineral that is a hydrous phosphate of copper and aluminium, with the chemical formula CuAl6(PO4)4(OH)8·4H2O. It is rare and valuable in finer grades and has been prized as a gemstone for millennia due to its hue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Turquoise-40031.jpg/330px-Turquoise-40031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turquoise"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/D-BRRR_%287493570480%29.jpg/500px-D-BRRR_%287493570480%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/D-BRRR_%287493570480%29.jpg/1280px-D-BRRR_%287493570480%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/D-BRRR_%287493570480%29.jpg/500px-D-BRRR_%287493570480%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AD-BRRR_(7493570480).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Diving_signal_cold.jpg/500px-Diving_signal_cold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Diving_signal_cold.jpg/500px-Diving_signal_cold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Signal \"cold\" – unofficial (except recommended by CMAS), it is nonetheless used by many schools of diving and propagated through diving websites as one of the more useful additional signals[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiving_signal_cold.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Goose_bumps.jpg/500px-Goose_bumps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Goose_bumps.jpg/500px-Goose_bumps.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Goose bumps, a common physiological response to cold, aiming to reduce the loss of body heat in a cold environment","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoose_bumps.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/AntarcticaDomeCSnow.jpg/500px-AntarcticaDomeCSnow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/AntarcticaDomeCSnow.jpg